<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariovistus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariovistus was a Germanic leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> who crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> around 70 BCE as an ally of the Sequani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>. After defeating the Aedui he settled his people on Sequanian land, and in 59 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> recognised him as king and friend. In 58 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> defeated him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and drove his army back across the Rhine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariovistus.jpg" alt="Ariovistus" width="216" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Johann Nepomuk Geiger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesar_and_Ariovistus_(meeting_before_the_battle)_by_Johann_Nepomuk_Geiger_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ariovistus is known only through Roman sources, chiefly books 1.31 to 1.54 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s Commentarii de Bello Gallico, and no non-Roman account survives. Modern historians read Caesar's portrait of him as a violent, rash and angry barbarian with caution, since the commentaries served as political propaganda for the Senate and the Roman public. According to Caesar, Ariovistus spoke Gaulish fluently and had two wives, a Suebian and a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voccio">King Voccio</a> of Noricum. Caesar styled him king of the Germani, though his authority probably extended only over the Germanic groups that had crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. In 59 BCE the Senate recognised him as king and friend of the Roman people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> and Sequani asked Ariovistus to help them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s allies, whom he had already defeated a few years earlier. At the Battle of Magetobriga his 15,000 men defeated the Aedui, took hostages and seized a third of their land, settling 120,000 Germanic people there. He then demanded more land for the Harudes, some 24,000 of them, and began to devastate Sequani lands. His forces were a migratory mix of peoples, among them the Harudes, Triboci and Marcomanni.</p><p>In 58 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> appealed to Caesar through Diviciacus. Caesar demanded that Ariovistus stop crossings, release hostages and respect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s allies, and Ariovistus refused. When they met with only cavalry, each accused the other of bad faith. Ariovistus manoeuvred around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Vesontio</a> to cut off Caesar's supplies, and the Germanic tribes, drawn up by tribe, finally faced Caesar's six legions. Germanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> tactics briefly pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> back until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-licinius-crassus">Publius Licinius Crassus</a> committed reserves. Ariovistus's line broke and fled toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. He escaped, but both his wives were killed, one daughter was slain and another captured. The site of the battle is uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Caesar's victory broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>'s ability to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> for years, and defeating both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> and Ariovistus in one year was, in Adrian Goldsworthy's words, a massive achievement. Ariovistus disappears from history soon afterwards; by 54 BCE Caesar noted that his death had provoked indignation among Germanic warriors. In the early Empire the groups that had fought for him appear on both banks of the Rhine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, forming the basis of Germania Superior, and over time they integrated into Romano-Celtic society. His name is usually derived from Gaulish elements meaning noble and knowledge, giving noble sage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariovistus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressed by the Sequani and <b>Ariovistus</b>, the Aedui called on Caesar in 58 BCE, but in 52 BCE they joined Vercingetorix, who was chosen supreme leader at Bibracte.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li><li><blockquote>The Marcomanni ('border men') first appear among <b>Ariovistus</b>' followers in 58 BCE, probably living near the upper Rhine and Main.</blockquote><a href="/state/marcomanni">Marcomanni</a></li><li><blockquote>To defeat the Aedui they hired the German leader <b>Ariovistus</b>, who then seized a third of their land, until Caesar drove him out in 58 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sequani">Sequani</a></li><li><blockquote>The Suebi enter history in 58 BCE when Julius Caesar fought their leader <b>Ariovistus</b> in Alsace, after he had crossed the Rhine about 72 BCE to aid the Sequani.</blockquote><a href="/state/suebi">Suebi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Triboci appear in Caesar's account of the battle against <b>Ariovistus</b> in 58 BCE as one of the peoples in his army.</blockquote><a href="/state/triboci">Triboci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=-35" aria-label="Germania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4802 398 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ariovistus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=-35">What was here in 35 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
